Actually, my proposed fix for genocide would be much
simpler, and I have a patch for NH4 that implements it.

Basically, with my patch, the game tracks how many 
individual monster types have been genocided so far, 
and each time you attempt to genocide one, it only 
succeeds if (rn2(genocount) * rn2(genocount) <= 25)

If you attempt an entire class (e.g., all L), it
goes through the list and does this check for each
individual monster type in the class.  Any time the
check fails, "Guilt overwhelms you, and you allow
some %s to escape", meaning the genocide fails to
happen for that monster type.  You can try again.

Players who are performing a reasonable number of 
genocides will not even notice this change, because 
it isn't even possible to get the failure until at
least the sixth type of monster.  (On the sixth one,
the odds of failure are 1 in 36, but they go up
from there as you genocide more and more monsters.)
This still leaves it technically possible to genocide
everything that's genocidable, but doing more than
about a dozen monster types would be extremely 
tedious and necessarily involve some kind of farming.

The intent is to encourage more selectivity and less
indiscriminate blessed nuking of multiple whole letters
of the alphabet.  You could still do all L -- there are
only four of those I think -- but if you do all L plus 
all h and then try to do all ; as well, you'll probably 
get some failures and need more than three scrolls
to get it all done.

